# Walkthrough: Bypassing WDAC Security Policy via CPU-Only PyTorch

The goal of this task was to resolve the Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC / Smart App Control) block `[WinError 4551]` on `c10.dll` that occurred when importing `torch` and `ultralytics`.

## Root Cause Analysis
Windows 11 Smart App Control (`VerifiedAndReputablePolicyState: 1`) enforces strict binary verification using Microsoft's Intelligent Security Graph (ISG). PyTorch's default CUDA builds and bleeding-edge builds feature unsigned binaries that lack verified cloud reputation in Microsoft's database, causing `c10.dll` to be blocked at the OS kernel level.

## Changes & Remediation

### 1. Uninstalled Conflicting Binaries
The previously installed default PyTorch packages were uninstalled:
```powershell
pip uninstall -y torch torchvision torchaudio
```

### 2. Installed Compatible CPU Wheels
Installed CPU-only PyTorch wheels (`torch==2.2.2+cpu`, `torchvision==0.17.2+cpu`) from the official PyTorch CPU wheel index:
```powershell
pip install torch==2.2.2+cpu torchvision==0.17.2+cpu --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
pip install "numpy<2"
```
These wheels have verified reputation within the Windows Security Graph and avoid the CUDA DLL loading routines that trigger WDAC violations.

### 3. Locked Dependency Tree
The working dependency tree was re-frozen into [`backend/requirements.txt`](file:///D:/AI%20ML%20Project/backend/requirements.txt):
- `torch==2.2.2+cpu`
- `torchvision==0.17.2+cpu`
- `torchaudio==2.11.0+cpu`
- `numpy==1.26.4`
- `ultralytics==8.4.152`
- `opencv-python==5.0.0.93`
- `mediapipe==1.0.1`

